---x--x--x 1 root root 40 Oct  8 19:33 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
[banner@stapp03 ~]$
I can see the issue now. The file permissions are ---x--x--x, which means:

Owner (root): --x (execute only — no read, no write)

Group: --x (execute only)

Others: --x (execute only)

The problem is that no one has READ permission on the file. Even though the execute bit is set, a shell script needs to be readable by the user running it (or by the shell/interpreter) to actually execute.

When you run bash /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh, bash tries to read the file — but it can't, hence "Permission denied."

🔧 The Fix
You need to add read permission for all users (or at least give read + execute):

bash
sudo chmod +rx /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
This adds read (r) and execute (x) for all users. The result will be:

text
-r-xr-xr-x 1 root root 40 Oct  8 19:33 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
Or, if you want to match the typical expected permission set (rwxr-xr-x):

bash
sudo chmod 755 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
Why chmod +x didn't work
chmod +x only adds the execute bit. Since the file already had x for all users, the command had no effect — it didn't touch the missing read bits.

✅ Verify and Test
bash
ls -l /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
cat /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
/tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
After the fix, you should see something like:

text
-r-xr-xr-x 1 root root 40 Oct  8 19:33 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
And the script should execute without errors.

📝 Summary
Command	Effect
chmod +x	Adds execute only (no effect if x already present)
chmod +rx	Adds read and execute for all users ✅
chmod 755	Sets rwxr-xr-x — full standard permissions ✅
chmod 555	Sets r-xr-xr-x — read+execute for all, no write
Run this to complete the task:

bash
sudo chmod 755 /tmp/xfusioncorp.sh
After conducting a security audit within the Stratos DC, the Nautilus security team discovered misconfigured permissions on critical files. To address this, corrective actions are being taken by the production support team. Specifically, the file named /etc/hosts on Nautilus App 2 server requires adjustments to its Access Control Lists (ACLs) as follows:



1. The file's user owner and group owner should be set to root.

2. Others should possess read only permissions on the file.

3. User mariyam must not have any permissions on the file.

4. User ryan should be granted read only permission on the file.
I'll help you configure the ACLs on /etc/hosts on Nautilus App 2 according to the specifications.

Steps to Complete the Task
1. SSH into Nautilus App 2
bash
ssh username@app-server-2
# Or use the specific hostname/IP provided in your environment
2. Set owner and group to root
bash
sudo chown root:root /etc/hosts
3. Set "others" to read-only permissions
Since others should have read-only, and typically owner (root) needs read+write for /etc/hosts, a common approach is 644:

bash
sudo chmod 644 /etc/hosts
Permission breakdown for 644:

Owner (root): rw- (read + write)

Group (root): r-- (read only)

Others: r-- (read only) ✅

⚠️ Note: If the requirement strictly means only others get read, you might use 600 or 400. However, 644 is standard for /etc/hosts since root must be able to modify it. I'll proceed with 644 — adjust if your task specifies otherwise.

4. Set ACL: mariyam has NO permissions
bash
sudo setfacl -m u:mariyam:--- /etc/hosts
This explicitly grants mariyam no permissions (overriding any group/default permissions).

5. Set ACL: ryan has read-only permission
bash
sudo setfacl -m u:ryan:r-- /etc/hosts
6. Verify the ACL configuration
bash
sudo getfacl /etc/hosts
Expected output should look like:

text
# file: etc/hosts
# owner: root
# group: root
user::rw-
user:mariyam:---
user:ryan:r--
group::r--
mask::r--
other::r--
Key checks:

✅ owner: root, group: root

✅ other::r-- (others read-only)

✅ user:mariyam:--- (mariyam no permissions)

✅ user:ryan:r-- (ryan read-only)

✅ One-Liner Sequence
bash
sudo chown root:root /etc/hosts && \
sudo chmod 644 /etc/hosts && \
sudo setfacl -m u:mariyam:--- /etc/hosts && \
sudo setfacl -m u:ryan:r-- /etc/hosts && \
sudo getfacl /etc/hosts
📝 Important Notes
About the ACL mask
When you set an ACL entry, the mask is automatically recalculated. The mask defines the maximum effective permissions for named users, named groups, and the owning group.

If your getfacl shows mask::---, then even ryan's r-- entry would be ineffective. To be safe, ensure the mask allows read:

bash
sudo setfacl -m m::r /etc/hosts
Then verify:

text
user:mariyam:---
user:ryan:r--
mask::r--
Removing mariyam's permissions entirely
If the requirement means mariyam should not appear in the ACL at all (rather than having explicit ---), you would instead remove the entry:

bash
sudo setfacl -x u:mariyam /etc/hosts
However, if mariyam is part of a group that has permissions, the explicit --- entry is the safer way to guarantee she has no access. The task wording ("must not have any permissions") is best satisfied with the explicit u:mariyam:--- entry.

Verify with ls -l
After setting ACLs, ls -l will show a + at the end of the permission string, indicating extended ACLs are present:

text
-rw-r--r--+ 1 root root ... /etc/hosts
🔍 Full Verification Checklist
bash
# Check ownership
ls -l /etc/hosts

# Check ACLs
getfacl /etc/hosts

# Test as mariyam (should fail)
sudo -u mariyam cat /etc/hosts

# Test as ryan (should succeed)
sudo -u ryan cat /etc/hosts
The task is complete once getfacl confirms all four requirements are met.

